      "worked": "<strong>Why swap by value fails — step by step</strong><br>In main: a = 10, b = 20<br><br><strong>Call swap(a, b):</strong> C creates two NEW variables x and y and copies into them<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;x = 10, y = 20 &nbsp;(a and b are untouched)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;temp = x = 10<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;x = y &rarr; x = 20<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;y = temp &rarr; y = 10<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Inside the function: x = 20, y = 10 &nbsp;<strong>(the swap worked here!)</strong><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;Function ends &rarr; x and y are destroyed<br><strong>Back in main: a = 10, b = 20 — NOTHING changed.</strong><br><br><strong>Call swap(&amp;a, &amp;b):</strong> the addresses are copied instead<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;x holds the address of a, y holds the address of b<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;temp = *x = 10 &nbsp;(reads the value at a's address)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;*x = *y &rarr; a becomes 20<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;*y = temp &rarr; b becomes 10<br><strong>Back in main: a = 20, b = 10 — the swap is permanent.</strong>",
      "code": "#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid swapByValue(int x, int y) {      /* gets COPIES */\n    int temp = x;\n    x = y;\n    y = temp;                         /* only the copies are swapped */\n}\n\nvoid swapByReference(int *x, int *y) { /* gets ADDRESSES */\n    int temp = *x;\n    *x = *y;\n    *y = temp;                        /* the originals are swapped */\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = 10, b = 20;\n\n    swapByValue(a, b);\n    printf(\"After value    : a = %d, b = %d\\n\", a, b);   /* 10, 20 - unchanged */\n\n    swapByReference(&a, &b);\n    printf(\"After reference: a = %d, b = %d\\n\", a, b);   /* 20, 10 - swapped */\n    return 0;\n}",
